.TH smi-make-diskless "1" "August 2026" "SMI Toolset" "SMI Diskless Command"
.SH NAME
smi-make-diskless \- Prepare a diskless installation as a chroot installation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smi-make-diskless
.I DISKLESS_NAME
.I SERVER_IP
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
.B smi-make-diskless
prepares a complete Linux installation in a directory of the server and
configures the server so that a diskless machine boots it over PXE, TFTP and
NFS.
The installation is a chroot installation: it can be entered, managed and
extended on the server at any time with the other diskless commands.
.PP
.I SERVER_IP
is the address of this server as the diskless client sees it.
It is used in the NFS mounts of the client and as the
.B next-server
of the DHCP server.
.PP
Running the command again for an existing installation updates it.
The configuration files of the server are changed in place, every command
replaces only its own block, so nothing is duplicated and nothing of the rest
of the file is lost.
.SH LAYOUT
.PP
Every installation is one directory below
.BR /var/opt/setmy.info/diskless :
.TP
.B NAME/root
The chroot, exported as the NFS root of the client.
.TP
.B NAME/home
Exported and mounted as
.B /home
of the client.
.TP
.B NAME/var
Exported and mounted as
.B /var
of the client.
Seeded from the chroot, so the client starts with the files the installation
created.
.TP
.B NAME/diskless.conf
Settings of the installation.
Written here and read by all other diskless commands, so they need the name
of the installation only.
.PP
The boot files of the client are below
.BI /var/lib/tftpboot/ NAME\fR.
.SH STEPS
.IP 1.
Installs
.BR dhcp-server ,
.BR tftp-server ,
.BR nfs-utils ,
.B dracut-network
and
.B rsync
on the server.
.IP 2.
Creates the directories of the installation.
.IP 3.
Installs the distribution into the chroot with
.BR "dnf --installroot" ,
from the distribution repositories only, so no third party repository of the
server leaks into the client.
.IP 4.
Generates the initramfs of the client in the chroot with
.BR dracut (8)
and the
.B nfs
and
.B network
modules.
The installer initramfs of the distribution is not usable for this, it starts
the installer instead of mounting an NFS root.
.IP 5.
Copies the kernel and that initramfs to the TFTP directory of the
installation.
.IP 6.
Configures the client: NFS entries in
.BR fstab ,
host name, empty machine id and disabled SELinux, which cannot keep its labels
on an NFS root.
.IP 7.
Configures the server: NFS exports, DHCP server and the boot menus.
.IP 8.
Enables the services and opens the firewall for DHCP, TFTP and NFS.
.SH BOOT
.PP
The boot menu is generated from the installations that exist, both for UEFI
clients
.RB ( grub.cfg )
and for legacy BIOS clients
.RB ( pxelinux.cfg/default ),
so the menu can never name an installation that is not there.
The DHCP server selects the boot file from the architecture option of the
client, so both firmware types are served by one server.
.PP
Legacy BIOS boot needs the
.B syslinux
package, which does not exist on every release any more.
When it is missing only UEFI clients are served, which is reported and is not
an error.
UEFI boot needs
.B shim-x64
and
.B grub2-efi-x64
on the server.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.PP
Every setting has a default and can be overridden in the environment.
.TP
.B DISKLESS_DIR
Root of the installations.
Default:
.BR /var/opt/setmy.info/diskless .
.TP
.B DISKLESS_TFTP_DIR
Root of the boot files.
Default:
.BR /var/lib/tftpboot .
.TP
.B DISKLESS_RELEASEVER
Release of the distribution installed into the chroot.
Default:
.BR 10 .
.TP
.B DISKLESS_REPOS
Repositories used for the chroot.
Default:
.BR baseos,appstream,extras .
.TP
.B DISKLESS_GROUPS
Package groups installed into the chroot.
Default:
.BR @core .
.TP
.B DISKLESS_PACKAGES
Packages installed into the chroot.
Default:
.BR "kernel dracut-network nfs-utils dbus openssh-server" .
.TP
.B DISKLESS_NFS_VERSION
NFS version of the client mounts.
Default:
.BR 4.2 .
.TP
.B DISKLESS_NFS_CLIENTS
Clients allowed in the exports.
Default:
.BR * .
.TP
.B DISKLESS_EXPORT_OPTIONS
Export options.
Default:
.BR rw,sync,no_root_squash,no_subtree_check .
.TP
.B DISKLESS_KERNEL_OPTIONS
Additional kernel options of the client.
Default:
.BR selinux=0 .
.TP
.B DISKLESS_DEFAULT_NAME
Installation of the default boot menu entry.
Default: the first installation.
.PP
The DHCP settings
.SM DISKLESS_DHCP_SUBNET ,
.SM DISKLESS_DHCP_NETMASK ,
.SM DISKLESS_DHCP_BROADCAST ,
.SM DISKLESS_DHCP_RANGE_FROM ,
.SM DISKLESS_DHCP_RANGE_TO ,
.SM DISKLESS_DHCP_ROUTER ,
.SM DISKLESS_DHCP_DNS ,
.SM DISKLESS_DHCP_DOMAIN ,
.SM DISKLESS_DHCP_LEASE_TIME
and
.SM DISKLESS_DHCP_MAX_LEASE_TIME
describe the network of the diskless machines.
The defaults are the
.B 10.0.0.0/8
network.
.SH "SETUP FROM ZERO"
.PP
The whole flow from an empty server to a running diskless machine.
The example prepares the installation
.B ws\-01
on a server with the address
.B 10.0.0.2
in the
.B 10.0.0.0/8
network.
.SS "1. The server"
.PP
The server needs a fixed address in the network of the diskless machines, and
enough space below
.B /var/opt
for every installation, a base installation is a few gigabytes.
The SMI toolset has to be installed, the diskless commands need nothing else.
The packages of the services are installed by this command.
.PP
The server has to be the only DHCP server of that network, otherwise the
diskless machines get their addresses from somewhere else and never find the
boot files.
.SS "2. Preparing the installation"
.PP
.RS
.nf
smi-make-diskless ws\-01 10.0.0.2
.fi
.RE
.PP
This installs the distribution into
.BR /var/opt/setmy.info/diskless/ws\-01/root ,
generates the initramfs of the client, writes the exports, the DHCP
configuration and the boot menus, and starts the services.
It takes a while, it is a full installation of the distribution.
.PP
When the network of the diskless machines is not
.BR 10.0.0.0/8 ,
give it in the environment:
.PP
.RS
.nf
DISKLESS_DHCP_SUBNET=192.168.1.0 \\
DISKLESS_DHCP_NETMASK=255.255.255.0 \\
DISKLESS_DHCP_BROADCAST=192.168.1.255 \\
DISKLESS_DHCP_RANGE_FROM=192.168.1.100 \\
DISKLESS_DHCP_RANGE_TO=192.168.1.200 \\
DISKLESS_DHCP_ROUTER=192.168.1.1 \\
DISKLESS_DHCP_DNS=192.168.1.1 \\
smi\-make\-diskless ws\-01 192.168.1.2
.fi
.RE
.SS "3. The users of the machine"
.PP
The installation has no password of any user yet, so nobody can log in.
.PP
.RS
.nf
smi\-diskless\-passwd ws\-01 root
smi\-diskless\-useradd ws\-01 has
smi\-diskless\-passwd ws\-01 has
.fi
.RE
.SS "4. The software of the machine"
.PP
Everything the machine needs is installed into the chroot on the server:
.PP
.RS
.nf
smi\-diskless\-install\-package ws\-01 @workstation
smi\-diskless\-install\-package ws\-01 vim git tmux
.fi
.RE
.PP
Anything the package commands cannot do is done in the chroot itself:
.PP
.RS
.nf
smi\-diskless\-chroot ws\-01 systemctl enable sshd
smi\-diskless\-chroot ws\-01
.fi
.RE
.PP
When a package command installed a new kernel, the initramfs of the client has
to be generated again for it and copied to the boot files, otherwise the
machine keeps booting the old one:
.PP
.RS
.nf
smi\-diskless\-chroot ws\-01 dracut \-\-force \-\-no\-hostonly \-\-add "nfs network" \\
    /boot/initramfs\-KERNEL_VERSION\-diskless.img KERNEL_VERSION
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B smi\-diskless\-list
prints the kernel version the installation was prepared with.
.SS "5. A fixed address for the machine"
.PP
A diskless machine may get an address from the range, but a fixed address per
machine is easier to work with.
The library function writes the block of one machine into the DHCP
configuration:
.PP
.RS
.nf
. $(smi\-include diskless.sh)
diskless_dhcp_host ws\-01 66:FB:8B:4F:AC:3A 10.0.0.11
smi\-start\-diskless
.fi
.RE
.SS "6. Booting the machine"
.PP
Set the network card as the first boot device in the firmware of the diskless
machine and start it.
It gets its address from the DHCP server of this server, loads the boot loader
and the menu over TFTP, and the entry of the installation mounts the NFS root.
.SS "7. When it does not boot"
.PP
.RS
.nf
smi\-diskless\-list                  # is the installation there
showmount \-e 10.0.0.2              # are the exports there
sudo journalctl \-u dhcpd \-f        # does the machine get an address
sudo journalctl \-u tftp \-f         # does it load the boot files
sudo journalctl \-u nfs\-server \-f   # does it mount the root
.fi
.RE
.PP
Restart everything after a change of the configuration of the server that was
not made by these commands:
.PP
.RS
.nf
smi\-start\-diskless
.fi
.RE
.SS "8. More machines"
.PP
Every further machine is one more installation, prepared the same way and
added to the same boot menu:
.PP
.RS
.nf
smi\-make\-diskless ws\-02 10.0.0.2
smi\-diskless\-list
.fi
.RE
.PP
An installation that is not needed any more is removed with everything that
belongs to it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
smi\-diskless\-remove ws\-02
.fi
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Prepare an installation and serve it from this server:
.RS
.B smi-make-diskless ws-01 10.0.0.2
.RE
.PP
Prepare an installation of another release in another network:
.RS
.nf
DISKLESS_RELEASEVER=9 \\
DISKLESS_DHCP_SUBNET=192.168.1.0 \\
DISKLESS_DHCP_NETMASK=255.255.255.0 \\
DISKLESS_DHCP_RANGE_FROM=192.168.1.100 \\
DISKLESS_DHCP_RANGE_TO=192.168.1.200 \\
smi-make-diskless ws-02 192.168.1.2
.fi
.RE
.PP
Add a graphical desktop and a user to a prepared installation:
.RS
.nf
smi-diskless-install-package ws-01 @workstation
smi-diskless-useradd ws-01 has
smi-diskless-passwd ws-01 has
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.BI /var/opt/setmy.info/diskless/ NAME
Directory of one installation.
.TP
.BI /var/lib/tftpboot/ NAME
Kernel and initramfs of one installation.
.TP
.B /etc/exports
NFS exports, one block per installation.
.TP
.B /etc/dhcp/dhcpd.conf
DHCP server configuration, written by this command.
.SH AUTHOR
.PP
Command written by Imre Tabur.
